## 2. What You CANNOT Predict (The Mathematical Boundaries)
Mathematics itself proves that certain patterns are fundamentally unpredictable. If a pattern falls into these categories, even the most advanced code will fail to project it: [5] 

* Cryptographic Keys and Tokens: Secure tokens (like session cookies or JWT inputs) utilize Pseudorandom Number Generators (PRNGs) fed by high-entropy hardware noise. Mathematically, a secure random sequence has no pattern; knowing the last 1,000 tokens gives you a $0\%$ advantage in guessing the next one. [6, 7] 
* True Chaos (Sensitive Dependence): In chaotic systems, the tiniest measurement error in your starting data compounds exponentially over time. This is known as the Butterfly Effect. If your initial logging metrics are off by even $0.0001\%$, your long-term calculus predictions will become completely inaccurate. [8, 9, 10, 11, 12] 
* Human Operational Choices: While you can predict general infrastructure trends, you cannot calculate the exact minute an administrator will decide to shut down a specific server or modify a firewall rule set.

